#### Java developers can now surf the genAI wave too

In my [last article on gRPC](https://medium.com/mitb-for-all/a-very-simple-introduction-to-grpc-6a666a039c03), I announced a hiatus from writing to focus on learning a new language: Java.

Beyond being a staple in every corporation, I noticed that generative AI frameworks are beginning to make inroads into Java. For example, the A2A protocol and Google’s ADK framework are now available in Java. Even Java’s ubiquitous Spring Boot — the FastAPI equivalent — now has a sibling called Spring AI. It’s only a matter of time before companies start requesting production systems built on these frameworks.

So imagine my surprise when I discovered that the top generative AI framework in Java is… [LangChain4j](https://github.com/langchain4j/langchain4j)! LangChain, already a game changer in Python, now has a Java counterpart, opening the door for Java developers to build generative AI applications more easily than ever before.

**There’s an important caveat: LangChain4j is not maintained by the official LangChain team — it’s a community-driven framework.** Nonetheless, it has built a strong following, with numerous integrations and an impressive 9.2k GitHub stars (the highest of any Java generative AI framework) to show for it.

In this article, I’ll walk you through using LangChain4j for chatting, structured output, and building a simple RAG application — all in Java. I’ll also share my observations and thoughts at the end, including a comparison between LangChain4j and its Python counterpart.

As with all my articles, the code is available in my [GitHub repository](https://github.com/tituslhy/LangChainJava).

### For those who have never coded in Java

The culture shock is quite huge.

The first line of the [Zen of Python](https://peps.python.org/pep-0020/) states, _“Beautiful is better than ugly.”_ Java has no such mantra. Its most famous line might be [_“Write once, run anywhere.”_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Write_once,_run_anywhere) As a result, Java code can take some getting used to.

This segment is meant to help you comprehend Java code — it is **not** a crash course in Java!

Java is a static typed language, which means you must declare your type at definition. For example:

```java
String name = "Titus";
```

Java is also a 100% object-oriented programming language, which means every method must live in a class. There are no utils.py files with just functions. Needless to say, all methods must also have their output types explicitly declared at definition.

Each `.java` file can contain only **one public class**, and this class **must have the same name as the file**. Other classes in the same file are non-public and can only be accessed by other classes in the same package.

In Java, classes can inherit from only one other class. To get around this limitation, Java has **interfaces** — contracts that classes can implement. Interfaces can contain abstract methods, which must be implemented by any class that implements them, and they can also contain concrete methods.

Java also has several access modifiers.

-   “Private” means that the attribute/method is only usable within the class.
-   “Protected” means that the attribute/method can be used by classes that inherit from it.
-   “Public” means that the attribute/method can be accessed by any other class. This is generally more common for methods but not very common for class attributes. Class attributes are typically defined as private with access to them limited by getter and setter methods.

So you could have such a class:

```java
/**
 * A base class
 */
abstract class Animal {
    /**
     * An abstract method
     */
    public abstract void move();
}

/**
 * An interface with abstract methods
 */
interface Flyable {
    void takeOff();
    void land();
    void fly();
}

public class Bird extends Animal implements Flyable {
    private String type;

    /**
    * A class constructor in Java.
    */
    public Bird(String type) {
        this.type = type;
    }

    @Override
    public void move() {
        System.out.println("Birds fly");
    }

    @Override
    public void takeOff() {
        System.out.println("Taking off...");
    }

    @Override
    public void land() {
        System.out.println("Landing...");
    }

    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Flying");
    }
}
```

Notice that all implemented methods are annotated with @Override — Java requires this to indicate you’re overriding an abstract method (unlike Python decorators).

Finally, every Java application needs a **main entry point**:

```java
public class Main{
    public static void main(String[] args){
      // Your code here
    }
}
```

The compiler looks for this method to start execution — unless you’re using a framework like Spring Boot, which manages entry points differently.

### Installing dependencies in Java

Most Java projects use **Maven** as the build tool. Dependencies are specified in a _Project Object Model_ (pom.xml) file. To get started with an existing project:

1.  Install Maven (`brew install maven` on Mac).
2.  Run `mvn clean install` in your terminal to build the project and pull in dependencies.
3.  As you add new dependencies to pom.xml, rerun `mvn clean install` to import them into your code.
4.  Instead of PyPi, use [Maven repository](https://mvnrepository.com/) to look for your dependencies. You can’t run `pip install <package name>` though. Instead, you’ll have to manually copy the package’s XML tag and paste it in your pom.xml file. Here’s an example XML tag (for [LangChain4j](https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/dev.langchain4j/langchain4j/1.8.0)):

```xml
<!-- https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/dev.langchain4j/langchain4j -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>dev.langchain4j</groupId>
    <artifactId>langchain4j</artifactId>
    <version>1.8.0</version>
</dependency>
```

