IntelliJ is one of the most caring IDEs for Java. IntelliJ imports things automatically for you as you code them out and makes suggestions on how to code better — and there’s no LLM behind the scenes here! This helps catch bugs before even running anything.

### Chatting with an LLM in LangChain4j

I’ve organized my codes into different methods directly under the `Main` class here for ease of reference.

```java
// imports

@Sl4j //Sl4j: Simple logging for java
public class Main{
    /**
     * Utility function to load OpenAI API key safely from
     * .env file
     * @return OpenAI API Key
     */
    public static String getApiKey(){
        Dotenv dotenv = Dotenv.load();
        return dotenv.get("OPENAI_API_KEY");
    }

    /**
     * Simple invocation of a query
     * @param query
     */
    public static void chat(String query){
        OpenAiChatModel model = OpenAiChatModel.builder()
                .apiKey(getApiKey())
                .modelName("gpt-5-nano")
                .build();
        System.out.println(model.chat(query));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
        chat("why is the sky blue?");
    }
}
```

The getApiKey method simply loads the `OPENAI_API_KEY` variable from the `.env` file. To chat with an LLM, you first have to instantiate an `OpenAiChatModel` object. We do this by chaining a few of the class’ methods of the`OpenAiChatModel` class, specifying our apiKey and model name. Finally, use `.chat(query)` to chat with our LLM.

We annotate the class with [Lombok’s](https://projectlombok.org/api/lombok/extern/slf4j/Slf4j) `[@Sl4j](https://projectlombok.org/api/lombok/extern/slf4j/Slf4j)` to see logs for the class as it runs.

> Notice that the keyword here is not `invoke` like in LangChain or LangGraph!

Now when we run this main file — we get to see the LLM’s response:

### Streaming an LLM

Streaming responses in Java is a bit more involved than the simple chat method.

Adding on to the same class:

```java
// imports

@Sl4j
public class Main{
    // ... codes from before

     /**
     * Stream invocation of a query
     * @param query
     */
    public static void stream(String query) {
        StreamingChatModel model = OpenAiStreamingChatModel.builder()
                .apiKey(getApiKey())
                .modelName("gpt-5-nano")
                .build();

        List<ChatMessage> messages = asList(
                systemMessage("You are a helpful assistant"),
                userMessage(query)
        );

        CompletableFuture<ChatResponse> futureResponse = new CompletableFuture<>();

        model.chat(messages, new StreamingChatResponseHandler() {
            @Override
            public void onCompleteResponse(ChatResponse chatResponse) {
                futureResponse.complete(chatResponse);
            }

            @Override
            public void onPartialResponse(String partialResponse) {
                System.out.print(partialResponse);
            }

            @Override
            public void onError(Throwable error) {
                futureResponse.completeExceptionally(error);
            }
        });

        futureResponse.join();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
        chat("why is the sky blue?");
        stream("why are leaves green?");
    }
}
```

Streaming requires specifying an implementation of the `StreamingChatModel` interface. `OpenAiStreamingChatModel` provides this implementation, which we construct similarly to the simple chat model.

In Python, streaming is usually done by yielding chunks from a generator. In Java, it works differently:

1.  We define a `CompletableFuture<ChatResponse>` to collect the streamed chunks.
2.  We pass in a list of messages (`List<ChatMessage>`).
3.  The `.chat()` method now requires a **callback class**. In Java, functions aren’t first-class objects, so we implement the `StreamingChatResponseHandler` interface with its abstract methods as our callback:

-   On partial response, we print the text.
-   On completion, we fulfill the future.
-   On error, we propagate the exception.

This approach mimics Python’s streaming generator pattern but follows Java’s class-and-interface conventions.

### Generating Structured Output

Structured output is one of the most important functions of any LLM application — LLM can respond in a preferred format, typically a Pydantic class in Python.

Since Java is fully object-oriented, implementing structured output is straightforward, but it works a bit differently than in Python.

We’ll first define the schema of interest:

```java
class KeyPoint{
    @Description("The key point in a few words")
    String keyPoint;

    @Description("The elaboration of the key point")
    String elaboration;
}

class KeyPoints {
    List<KeyPoint> keyPoints;
}
```

Here, we want to extract a list of key points from a chunk of text, in a point-elaboration format. **Remember to annotate your class attributes with** `**@Description**` so the LLM understands the fields.

This is the part where it gets a bit strange. The structured output operation is defined as an interface:

```java
interface KeyPointExtractor{
    @UserMessage("Extract the key points from the following text: {{text}}")
    KeyPoints extractKeyPointsFrom(@V("text") String text);
}
```

-   The `extractKeyPointsFrom` method is abstract and takes a `String` text as input.
-   The `@UserMessage` annotation contains a placeholder (`{{text}}`) that will be substituted when calling the method.
-   The `@V(“text”)` annotation ensures the model knows which variable to extract from the user message.

#### Why an interface?

LangChain4j is designed around interfaces. You don’t implement the interface manually; instead, LangChain4j provides an abstract class (`AiServices`) that consumes your interface and implements the methods for you behind the scenes.

This pattern also extends to agents in LangChain4j — you define your agent as an interface and build it up by chaining methods over `AiServices` . In my opinion, the `AiServices` abstract class is one of the framework’s most important classes.

#### Using the KeyPointExtractor

Here’s how you can extract key points from a block of text:

```java
@Sl4j
public class Main{
    // ... codes from before

    /**
     * Structured output. Extract key points from a string of text
     * @param text
     */
    public static void getKeyPoints(String text){
        KeyPointExtractor extractor = AiServices.create(
                KeyPointExtractor.class,
                OpenAiChatModel.builder()
                        .apiKey(getApiKey())
                        .modelName("gpt-5-nano")
                        .build()
        );
        KeyPoints result = extractor.extractKeyPointsFrom(text);

        result.keyPoints.forEach(
                kp -> System.out.println(kp.keyPoint + " - " + kp.elaboration)
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
        // ...codes from before
        String importantText = """
                Stretching keeps the muscles flexible and healthy, and we need that flexibility to maintain a range of
                motion in the joints. Without it, the muscles shorten and become tight. Then, when you call on the
                muscles for activity, they are unable to extend all the way. That puts you at risk for joint pain,
                strains, and muscle damage.

                For example, sitting in a chair all day results in tight hamstrings in the back of the thigh. That can
                make it harder to extend your leg or straighten your knee all the way, which inhibits walking.
                Likewise, when tight muscles are suddenly called on for a strenuous activity that stretches them, such
                as playing tennis, they may become damaged from suddenly being stretched. Injured muscles may not be
                strong enough to support the joints, which can lead to joint injury.

                Regular stretching keeps muscles long, lean, and flexible, and this means that exertion won't put too
                much force on the muscle itself. Healthy muscles also help a person with balance problems avoid falls.

                With a body full of muscles, the idea of daily stretching may seem overwhelming. It's most important to
                focus on the body areas needed for critical for mobility: your lower extremities: your calves, your
                hamstrings, your hip flexors in the pelvis and quadriceps in the front of the thigh. Stretching your
                shoulders, neck, and lower back is also beneficial. Aim for a program of daily stretches, or at least
                three or four times per week.
                """;
        getKeyPoints(importantText);
    }

}
```

In the `getKeyPoints` method, we implement the `KeyPointsExtractor` interface using LangChain4j’s `AiServices` — this abstract class is responsible for a lot of interface implementation. The `AiServices`class consumes the interface and connects it to an LLM, which we construct from scratch.

Interestingly, the extractor variable automatically provides an implementation of the `extractKeyPointsFrom` abstract method — without us writing any additional code. Under the hood, LangChain4j uses function calling in JSON Schema format to handle this.

Finally, to display the results, we loop through the list of key points with forEach, using a Java lambda expression to format the output.

### Building a simple retrieval augmented generation app

This part wasn’t as straightforward as I hoped — mainly because the LangChain4j documentation wasn’t very illustrative. The examples mostly read .txt files, which wasn’t what I needed.

So I defined my RAG application as a public class in a separate .java file, and let’s walk through it step by step.

#### Constructors

```java
// RAG.java

//imports ...

interface Assistant {
    String chat(String userMessage);
}

@Sl4j
public class RAG {
    private OllamaEmbeddingModel embeddingModel;
    private OllamaChatModel llm;
    private ContentRetriever retriever;
    private Assistant assistant;

    public RAG(String directoryPath, int maxResults, double minScore){
        this.embeddingModel = OllamaEmbeddingModel.builder()
                .baseUrl("http://localhost:11434")
                .modelName("mxbai-embed-large:latest")
                .build();
        this.llm = OllamaChatModel.builder()
                .baseUrl("http://localhost:11434")
                .modelName("qwen2.5:latest")
                .timeout(Duration.ofMinutes(10))
                .build();

        // Chunk
        System.out.println("Chunking documents");
        PathMatcher pathMatcher = FileSystems.getDefault().getPathMatcher("glob:*.pdf");
        DocumentParser pdfParser = new ApachePdfBoxDocumentParser();
        List<Document> documents = FileSystemDocumentLoader.loadDocuments(directoryPath, pathMatcher, pdfParser);

        // Ingest
        System.out.println("Generating embeddings");
        InMemoryEmbeddingStore<TextSegment> embeddingStore = new InMemoryEmbeddingStore<>();
        EmbeddingStoreIngestor ingestor = EmbeddingStoreIngestor.builder()
                .embeddingModel(embeddingModel)
                .embeddingStore(embeddingStore)
                .build();
        System.out.println("Ingesting embeddings");
        ingestor.ingest(documents);

        this.retriever = EmbeddingStoreContentRetriever.builder()
                .embeddingStore(embeddingStore)
                .embeddingModel(this.embeddingModel)
                .maxResults(maxResults)
                .minScore(minScore)
                .build();
        RetrievalAugmentor retrievalAugmentor = DefaultRetrievalAugmentor.builder()
                .contentRetriever(retriever)
                .build();
        this.assistant = AiServices.builder(Assistant.class)
                .chatModel(llm)
                .chatMemory(MessageWindowChatMemory.withMaxMessages(10))
                .retrievalAugmentor(retrievalAugmentor)
                .build();
    }

    public RAG(){
        this(
                "src/main/resources",
                4,
                0.5
        );
    }
```

A constructor in Java is simply a method with the same name as the class. Java allows overloading, so we can have multiple constructors with different argument signatures.

We have 2 constructors defined above – one where the user has to specify all arguments and one where the user specifies none (so we use defaults). Overloaded constructors can call each other using the special keyword “this”. We used the no args constructor to call the all args constructor with default arguments to construct the class.

The RAG class has a few key components:

1.  An `Assistant` interface that will be implemented by another LangChain4j class,
2.  An Ollama embedding model and an Ollama LLM, and
3.  A retriever — this is the vector store.

On construction, we instantiate each part:

-   We build the embedding model and the LLM by chaining class methods as before.
-   Then we proceed to read the PDF files in the folder specified.
-   We parse the PDF using an `ApachePdfBoxDocumentParser` object, and chunk our document.
-   We then create an in-memory vector store by chaining class methods, and generate and ingest the vectors into it using an `EmbeddingStoreIngestor` class that takes the vector store, embedding models and document chunks.
-   We then set the retriever using class methods again, specifying top k chunks and minimum vector similarity scores.
-   We then use LangChain4j’s `DefaultRetrievalAugmentor` class to consume the retriever, and
-   Finally pass everything into LangChain4j’s `AiServices` abstract class again. Notice that we added `chatMemory` of up to the last 10 messages here.

The second constructor is just a “default args” constructor. It lets us spin up the RAG app without specifying anything.

#### To chat with your documents

```java
public String askChatbot(String query){
        System.out.printf("Received a query: %s \nGenerating response...%n", query);
        return assistant.chat(query);
    }
```

#### Creating our application

We’ll create a simple command line application to chat with our documents by invoking the RAG class.

```java
package com.langchain.example.rag;

import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;

import java.util.Scanner;

@Sl4j
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RAG chatbot = new RAG();
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Hello! Ask me any question on the GEPA framework! Say 'end' to end the conversation");
        while (true){
            String query = scanner.nextLine();
            if (query.equalsIgnoreCase("end")){
                System.out.println("Goodbye!");
                break;
            }
            String response = chatbot.askChatbot(query);
            System.out.println(response);
        }
    }
}
```

`Scanner` reads user input from CLI, and the while-loop simulates a running chatbot session. We use a while loop to simulate a conversation, and break it if the user types in “end”.

### Initial thoughts on Java for generative AI

Java engineers can finally dive into generative AI without switching languages. You really _can_ build full GenAI applications in pure Java now. And that’s genuinely exciting. Imagine an entire genAI backend application using LangChain4j with Spring Boot in one repo!

But I’ll be honest: I’ll still reach for Python unless the application is small or the environment forces Java. LangChain4j is fundamentally different from LangChain — not just in API design, but in mindset.

-   LangChain’s evolution went from **Chains → Runnables → StateGraphs**.
-   LangChain4j began with Chains but has since leaned fully into a **Java-native, interface-heavy, strongly typed** design.

The result? LangChain developers will feel a bit disoriented at first. The mental model is just different.

Take something simple: middleware for context engineering — a core concept introduced in LangChain 1.0 discussed in my [previous LangChain article](https://medium.com/mitb-for-all/langchain-a-second-look-6ed720e27fec). It doesn’t exist at all in LangChain4j. Meanwhile, Python frameworks like LlamaIndex, CrewAI, Agno, etc. have already implemented similar patterns.

And honestly, this shouldn’t surprise anyone familiar with the ecosystem.

[LangChain (Python) sits at roughly **120k GitHub stars**](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain) — several orders of magnitude above LangChain4j — because it’s at the center of the GenAI universe. It has a huge contributor base, a massive community, and an ecosystem of complementary libraries that all speak the same “Python-first” language.

Python itself plays a big role here: it’s more expressive, more flexible, and far easier for rapid experimentation… even if Java developers will defend their language over coffee.

As I built with LangChain4j, the philosophical differences became even clearer. LangChain4j feels like it was built **by software engineers for software engineers** — explicit, structured, heavily typed.

In contrast, most generative-AI innovation historically comes from **data scientists**, which is why the Python ecosystem has so much more interoperability and shared conventions.

Not to mention it took me much longer to code this out — though a part of this is because I’m still new to Java.

Java _is_ catching up, and LangChain4j is a meaningful step in that direction. But it still lacks many of the abstractions, patterns, and community norms that Python frameworks have refined over the years. The gap is narrowing. But right now, you can definitely feel the difference when you code in it.

Nonetheless, LangChain4j is a well-designed framework — arguably the most abstraction-rich GenAI library available in the Java ecosystem today. For LangGraph lovers, there is also [LangGraph4j](https://github.com/langgraph4j/langgraph4j)!

In contrast, [Google’s ADK Java repository hasn’t crossed 1k GitHub stars](https://github.com/google/adk-java), which suggests far lower real-world adoption. [Spring AI’s framework has 7.2k GitHub stars](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-ai) but [Spring Boot (by the same organization and the staple in developing backends in Java) has 79k GitHub stars](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot). Java feels more like a sword than a swiss army knife.

But, of course, it certainly doesn’t hurt that LangChain4j carries the LangChain name — a name that already commands attention in the GenAI world.

